<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="automation_write_pass">

  <!-- Proposal 37 P6, AC2: A TOUCH PASS IS ONE UNDO STEP, AND IT IS HEARD.

       Touch, Latch and Write are not engine modes — the engine reads a
       Read-family lane and cannot tell them apart. They are UI RECORDERS
       (design D5): a control feeds values in while the transport runs, the
       recorder buffers them on the UI thread, and the WHOLE PASS lands as ONE
       `set-automation-points` when the pass ends. That single-action property
       is the thing most easily got wrong and the thing this case exists for —
       a recorder that submitted an action per block would put thirty entries a
       second on the undo stack and a single Ctrl-Z would peel one 33 ms sliver
       off a four-second pass. Here THREE ticks are reverted by ONE `undo`.

       The ticks arrive through `automation-write-tick`, which is the
       `slip-clip` shape: it feeds the recorder and pushes no undo step of its
       own, exactly as a fader moving mid-pass does. It REJECTS on a lane that
       is not in touch/latch/write, so a case cannot pass by ticking into the
       void.

       THE TRANSPORT IS REAL. The capture audio backend is the default under
       a test-case run and paces on a real-time clock (SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED is
       1 unless set, and this case needs it to be), so `wait-playhead` is
       waiting on an actual playhead and the ticks land during actual
       playback. The pass ends where the design says it does: at the TRANSPORT
       STOP, from SApplication::setPlaying(false), on the main thread.

       ================================================================ audio

       Fixture ../test_autosaw.wav (tests/tools/gen_auto_fixture.py): 4.0 s,
       48 kHz, a 480 Hz sawtooth of amplitude 0.4 whose period is EXACTLY 100
       frames — so 100 divides 48000 and every whole second has the same rms,
       0.230956, with no window-alignment term at all.

       The pass writes, in dB and linear IN dB (P5's reading of the fader
       space): (0 s, −60) already on the lane, then (0.5 s, −40), (1.0 s, −20),
       (1.5 s, 0) and flat 0 dB afterwards. Integrating 10^(dB/10) over each
       second and multiplying by the fixture's own rms gives

           second 0   0.00760974
           second 1   0.18001059
           second 2   0.23095600
           second 3   0.23095600

       The bands below on seconds 0 and 1 are widened to cover a ±8192-frame
       capture-alignment slop (the same budget playback_start_after_edit_position
       measured and then paid for explicitly), which is why they are wide —
       they are wide in the direction the RAMP moves, not in the direction the
       feature would fail. They still separate everything that matters: with no
       pass at all the lane holds −60 dB everywhere and second 1 reads
       0.000231, a factor of 600 below its band; with the pass but no lane
       consumption every second reads 0.230956, a factor of 12 above second 0's.

       Seconds 2 and 3 are the flat 0 dB tail: after 1.5 s the curve IS unity,
       so anything that leaked the ramp into the tail (an invalidation that
       stopped at the last point instead of running to the end of time) shows
       up there. Their band is −10 %/+3 %, asymmetric on purpose — a momentary
       underrun leaves a zero gap and can only pull an rms DOWN, and the pump
       is paced by a real-time clock that deliberately does not wait for the
       readahead. −10 % still catches every leak worth catching: a tail left at
       −20 dB reads 0.0231, a factor of ten below the floor.

       MEASURED on this machine, first run: 0.00760879 / 0.18000646 /
       0.23095626 / 0.23091878 against the closed form above — five significant
       digits, and a capture-alignment slop of effectively zero frames. The
       bands are therefore far wider than this machine needs; they are sized
       for the machine that drops a block under `ctest -j4`, not for this one.
  -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- The lane, in TOUCH. One point at 0 s so the state the undo returns to
         is exact rather than "no lane". -->
    <add-automation-lane owner="0" target="self:Volume" mode="touch">
      <p t="0" v="-60" c="linear"/>
    </add-automation-lane>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="0" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"
                             mode="touch" pointCount="1"/>

    <!-- Before: the lane holds -60 dB at all three tick positions. These are
         the assertions the undo at the end has to restore. -->
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="24000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="48000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="72000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"/>

    <!-- ==================== THE PASS ====================
         Play from 0 and feed the recorder at 0.5 s, 1.0 s and 1.5 s. `time` is
         given explicitly so the POINTS are exact while the transport is still
         genuinely running (wait-playhead is what makes it so) — a tick that
         took the live locator instead would make every number below a
         measurement of this machine's scheduling. -->
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>

    <wait-playhead position="24000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="20000"/>
    <automation-write-tick owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                           time="24000" value="-40"/>

    <wait-playhead position="48000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="20000"/>
    <automation-write-tick owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                           time="48000" value="-20"/>

    <wait-playhead position="72000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="20000"/>
    <automation-write-tick owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                           time="72000" value="0"/>

    <wait-playhead position="96000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="20000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>

    <!-- The stop committed the pass. All three ticks are on the lane, plus the
         point that was already there. -->
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="24000" value="-40" tolerance="1e-9"
                             pointCount="4"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="48000" value="-20" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="72000" value="0" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <!-- ...as a CURVE, not three isolated values: the midpoint of the
         (0.5 s, -40) -> (1.0 s, -20) segment is -30 dB, and Touch released the
         lane at the last tick, so the tail holds 0 dB rather than returning to
         the -60 the lane started at. -->
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="36000" value="-30" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="180000" value="0" tolerance="1e-9"/>

    <!-- ==================== ...AND IT IS HEARD ====================
         A second real playback pass, from 0, all the way through. The capture
         backend restarts its recording at every playback start, so the dump is
         this pass and nothing else. -->
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead position="190000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="40000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>
    <dump-playback-capture filename="pass.wav" minFrames="184320"/>

    <assert-audio-energy filename="pass.wav" channel="0"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0031" maxRms="0.0184"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="pass.wav" channel="0"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.1375" maxRms="0.2236"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="pass.wav" channel="0"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.20786" maxRms="0.23788"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="pass.wav" channel="0"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="40000"
                         minRms="0.20786" maxRms="0.23788"/>

    <!-- ==================== ONE UNDO ====================
         THE headline: a single undo step takes back the entire pass. All three
         ticks are gone and the lane is exactly what it was. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="24000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"
                             pointCount="1"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="48000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="72000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"/>
    <assert-automation-value owner="0" target="self:Volume"
                             time="180000" value="-60" tolerance="1e-9"
                             mode="touch"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
